  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nUL high continues to dominate our weather, centered over the Four\nCorners. It has shifted far enough away to allow a modest \nincrease in moisture as well as cool the atmosphere a bit. This \ncombined with a small MCV allowed for an increase in convection \nacross the entire CWA. UL highs looks to move little with little \nchange in PW, so expect Friday to feature similar conditions. HREF\nand other guidance agrees with this. \n\nAs we go into Saturday, the UL high will drag drier air into the \nCWA with dewpoints dropping below 50 and PWs under an inch. \nTemperatures will warm a few degrees while storm chances drop, \nlimited to areas west of the Continental Divide. Saturday will \nfeature our lowest storm chances, limited to the Gila Wilderness. \nWith the source region of our air out of the northeast, \ntemperatures will not be too hot but still close to 100 for the \nlowlands.\n\nMid-week, Wednesday or Thursday, the UL high over the Four \nCorners rapidly weakens with a new high becoming dominate over the\nGulf of America coast. This will open the CWA to southerly flow \nand a true monsoonal pattern. Thunderstorm chances will increase \naccordingly.",
